  "version": "1.0",
  "modelName": "ReThink Health — the shape of it",
  "modelNote": "A readable redraw of Figure 1 of the ReThink Health Dynamics Reference Guide (Homer, Model Version 3v, Nov 2018). Their own overview diagram carries about 60 nodes with arrows crossing in every direction — a reference for modellers, not an image a group can argue about. This keeps the causal spine, the three feedback loops the guide names in prose, and the funding circulation that is the whole point of the model for a steward, and drops everything else.\n\nRead it as one counterclockwise circulation. Along the top, the spine runs left to right: disadvantage raises risk, risk raises chronic illness, illness produces acute episodes, episodes and illness drive utilization, utilization drives cost. On the right, cost turns into savings measured against a benchmark. Savings flow left into the fund. The fund pushes up into four families of intervention. The interventions push up into the spine. Then round again.\n\nThe three feedback loops are the ones the guide states in prose on page 1: rising cost pushes employers to drop coverage, which cuts insurance coverage; rising cost drives personal indebtedness, which raises disadvantage; and chronic illness causes disability, which also raises disadvantage. All three are vicious — they are why the system does not simply drift back to health on its own.\n\nTwo things the drawing deliberately shows. The delay marks on the two upstream arrows are real: environmental hazards, crime, and the pathways programs all carry 5-year time constants in the model, while every clinical and delivery intervention implements in 1 year. Any scenario judged on a short horizon will favour downstream work for reasons of timing alone. And the fund is a stock, not a tap — the guide is explicit that if funding ceases and accumulated funds deplete, gains made to date erode and eventually erode entirely.\n\nOne tension is named here but not drawn, because a second opposing arrow between the same pair makes the picture unreadable: medical homes pull patients toward primary care and the model warns this can create a PCP shortage unless paired with recruitment or practice redesign. Interventions in this model have to be combined, not chosen singly.\n\nParameters for all 23 interventions, with min-max uncertainty ranges and sources, are in docs/rth-intervention-parameters.md. Drawn Aug 17 2026.",
